              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "# Loaded: lore-self-career-index/references/business-disposition.md\n\n# business disposition\n\nKai's interest in building a business predates the current agent-tooling cycle. The relevant prior is CollectQT / Quirell, a queer and trans social-network prototype she built in 2014 and 2015.\n\n## Why the prior matters\n\nKai reads that attempt as constrained by solo development throughput, not by a lack of conviction in the problem. Current coding agents and a decade of skill growth change that constraint materially. The founding impulse is a long-running disposition. New tools make another attempt more feasible, but the tools did not create the disposition.\n\n## Product type before financing form\n\nKai starts with the thing worth creating. Bootstrapped product, productized service, contracting, or venture financing follow from the product's capital and scale needs. Some products force a financing form. Most do not need that decision before the problem and user are concrete.\n\n## Founding as a transition state\n\nA second and distinct motive sits alongside the founding impulse. Founding a company produces a credential that hiring teams read directly, and senior hiring conversations quote founder experience as evidence that a team is strong. The line carries weight independent of whether the company succeeds.\n\nKai holds this as a live option: found something while expecting it to fail, and take the credential and operating experience as the return.\n\n**Keep the two motives separate.** A business pursued because the problem is worth solving is evaluated by the rule below. A business pursued as a transition state is evaluated on whether the credential and experience are worth the time and money at risk. **Name which of the two is in play instead of dressing a transition-state attempt in conviction language.**\n\n## Evaluation rule\n\nGoverns a business pursued as a business. When an idea looks like a candidate, test:\n\n1. whether Kai cares about the problem beyond the novelty of building it\n2. whether current throughput changes the earlier bottleneck\n3. whether the product itself forces a particular capital form\n4. what evidence from a new build would update the throughput estimate\n\n**Do not promote a possible path into an established company, customer base, or offering without evidence.**\n\nRelated: `lore-self-credential-legibility` for the container the transition-state motive is answering.\n\n\n---\nFile loaded into context."
                }
              ],
              "isError": false
            }
          }
        }
      ],
