as a verdict.\"\n\nI DO have a decision model: Jev. So I should use it.\n\nLet me structure this:\n\n1. **Case against first** (my own words, sourced outside the proposal):\n   - The strongest case against anyone buying: acompose and umbra are open-source, not-yet-established, no customer base (portfolio explicitly forbids claiming one). The buyer for a \"custom cut + support\" of an agent-occlusion engine is a team already running agents against production systems — a thin, technical slice. mcp-beaver (the guarded-MCP piece) was already closed as a product because Kong AI Gateway 3.13 shipped MCP Tool ACLs, meaning the core \"constrain what an agent can do\" value has a free/commodity alternative in the market. Also, umbra \"ships no denylist because the policy is yours\" — but that means the \"custom cut\" is basically config + integration, which is the kind of thing a buyer might just do in-house or get from a generalist. And acompose's value (\"materialize a specialist\") is being commoditized by every harness vendor shipping personalities/agents.\n   - Also: these are single-maintainer, no LICENSE clarity in some repos (Agent Proxy row has no LICENSE). A support contract from a solo operator is a real supply-risk for a buyer.\n\n2. **Reversal condition:**\n   - What would flip \"no one buys\" to \"someone buys\": a buyer who is already paying for agent-infrastructure procurement and has a compliance/audit obligation (federal/defense chain, or a security team needing to prove what an agent touched) AND who cannot use off-the-shelf ACL tooling because they need policy-as-code at the execve boundary. The NDAA covered-AI angle: umbra ships no model, so it's sayable in defense rooms — that's a specific buyer. If a single warm introduction converts to a paid pilot, the \"no one buys\" flips.\n\n3. **Verdict via decision model:** Ask Jev:\n   - A noul (yes/no) question: \"Would at least one paying buyer exist for a custom cut + support of umbra within [timeframe]?\" Actually the question is \"would anyone actually buy one\" — two separate yes/no (one per product) or a score.\n   - A score question for the price bands? Or I derive price bands from the confirmed rate and mark the hours as estimates.\n\nLet me think about how to handle the price bands cleanly.\n\nPrice = hours × $150. The only \"estimate\" is hours. I can present:\n- One-time cut price bands as a function of scope in hours, clearly tied to the $150 rate.\n- The hours per scope bucket are my estimate (I should mark them or put them to the model).\n\nActually, I think the cleaner approach given the guardrail:\n\n- The **price bands** are deterministic arithmetic from the confirmed rate once I pick hour ranges. The hour ranges are the \"duration\" estimate. I should either put the hour ranges to the decision model as a score question, or clearly mark them as my own estimate.\n\n- The **buy/no-buy** is a go/no-go that must go to the model.\n\nLet me actually use Jev. Let me formulate:\n\n**Question 1 (score):** \"For a custom one-time cut of umbra (occlusion engine: argv/execve policy, per-verb scope tokens, egress proxy, audit log, exit-code taxonomy) delivered as professional services by a solo operator at a $150/hr rule rate, what is a realistic total hours band?\" — Actually, this is getting too in the weeds. The model won't know better than me about hours.\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider. The guardrail's intent is: numeric estimates (price, count, duration) and go/no-go go to the decision model. But the decision model (Jev) is a typed decision — it needs criteria/options. For price bands, a \"score\" question with criteria like \"one-time cut hours low bound\" doesn't map cleanly.\n\nActually, maybe the more sensible reading: the key decision-model-worthy call here is the **go/no-go**: \"Would anyone actually buy a custom cut + support of umbra? of acompose?\" That's a noul (yes/no). And the price bands are arithmetic I can compute from the confirmed $150 rate, with my 