n2. Do the outward searches (a few calls).\n3. Formulate Jev questions and call Jev for the verdicts (noul for buy/no-buy on each, maybe a score for price band or likelihood).\n4. Write the final answer: case against first, reversal condition, model verdict, price bands as arithmetic from confirmed rate + marked hour estimates.\n\nLet me start by loading web-search and checking network.\n\nActually, let me first check: is there a `web-search` tool I can call? The skills section lists \"web-search\" among many skills. Skills are loaded via load_skill. So the skill probably contains instructions for using a search tool or MCP. Let me load it.\n\nLet me also reconsider the Jev tooling. The kai-server_30089_mcp tools are available. And there's a \"kai-server_30089_mcp\" extension with create_noul_decision etc. Also \"mcp-tools-tailnet-coilyco-jev\" skill mentions \"Jev (TypeSafe System One) typed decisions through Agent Proxy - one tool each for noul, choice, and score.\"\n\nThe available function tools include kai-server_30089_mcp__create_noul_decision, create_choice_decision, create_score_decision. Good. These are the decision model.\n\nLet me figure out the model name. The kai-server_30089_mcp instructions say \"model is jev-latest (or jev-1.13.0, jev-preview). state is required.\"\n\nSo I'll use model \"jev-latest\".\n\nNow, let me design the Jev questions carefully per the guardrail:\n- Neutral questions.\n- State carries primary sources + evidence on both sides, never my verdict.\n- Options symmetric, no encoded answer.\n\nFor the buy/no-buy, I'll do a noul (yes/no) question. Actually, \"would anyone actually buy\" is a yes/no. But there are two products. I can do two noul questions, or one noul per product.\n\nActually, let me think about what's most decision-useful. The user wants:\n- Price bands for each (acompose, umbra).\n- Whether anyone would buy (per each, or overall).\n\nI'll do:\n- noul question 1: umbra buy demand\n- noul question 2: acompose buy demand\n\nAnd maybe a score question for the price band magnitude (low/medium/high). But honestly, the price band is arithmetic from rate × hours, and the hours are scope-dependent. I can present price bands without the model, clearly marking hours as my estimate. The model is for the go/no-go.\n\nActually, re-reading the guardrail once more: \"Every probability, likelihood, numeric estimate (a price, a count, a duration), ranking, and go or no-go goes to the deployment's decision model as a typed question.\"\n\nSo a \"price\" is explicitly named. Hmm. But \"arithmetic that is deterministic stays local.\" The price is deterministic from rate × hours. But hours is the non-deterministic part.\n\nI think the cleanest: I'll put the buy/no-buy (go/no-go) to the model — that's clearly required and load-bearing. For price, I'll present it as: confirmed rate $150/hr, and I'll give hour-band estimates for the scope, explicitly marked as my own estimate, and I can also put a \"what's the realistic hours band for a custom one-time cut\" as a score question to the model. Actually that's reasonable — a score question with criteria like \"5-15 hours\", \"15-40 hours\", \"40-100 hours\", \"100+ hours\" — but that's a choice, not a score.\n\nHmm, let me think about what Jev's score tool is for: \"score is an ordered level.\" So score = ordered levels (e.g., low/med/high, or 1-5). Not great for hours.\n\nThe choice tool: \"choice picks one option with why.\" So choice could ask \"what's the realistic scope band for a one-time cut\" with options.\n\nThe noul tool: yes/no.\n\nGiven the complexity, let me keep it focused:\n\n1. **noul** for umbra: \"Is there a paying buyer for a custom cut + long-term support of umbra?\"\n2. **noul** for acompose: same.\n3. Optionally a **choice** for each product's price band, with symmetric options (low/mid/high ranges), so the price number also comes from the model.\n\nActually, I think the most honest and useful approach given constraints:\n\n- Present the price bands as my computed arithmetic (rate × hours) and clearly mark the hours as my estimate — this is transparent and the guardrail explicitly allows marking as \"this seat's own unchecked estimate\" when the call isn't sent to the model.\n- But actually I CAN send it to the model. Let me send the buy/no-buy to the model (noul ×2), and send the price-band magnitude as a choice question ×2 (with symmetric options), so both the \"would anyone buy\" AND the \"how much\" come from the model.\n\nBut wait — Jev \"takes no images and answers only the typed shape it was asked.\" And \"Not for computer use or freeform generation.\" So Jev won't produce a dollar number; it picks from options or scores levels. So for price, I'd give it option bands as a choice and it picks one. That satisfies \"price goes to the model.\"\n\nLet me design symmetric options for price band choice. For each product, the \"one-time cut + long-term support\" total first-year price band. Options should be symmetric, not encoding my expectation.\n\nActually, this is getting heavy. Let me simplify to what's decision-grade:\n\nThe user asked two things: (1) price bands, (2) would anyone buy. \n\nLet me do the outward search first (to have market context), then Jev for the buy/no-buy (the load-bearing verdict), and present price bands as arithmetic + clearly-marked estimates, referencing the confirmed rate.\n\nFor the price bands, I'll define scope in hours and multiply by $150. I'll be transparent: the rate is confirmed; the hours are my estimate of scope; the retainer is my estimate.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the guardrail severity. It says price and duration go to the model. I have the model. To be compliant AND useful, I'll:\n\n1. WebSearch/outward check for market context (do I have the tool? need to check).\n2. Jev noul for buy/no-buy on each product.\n3. Present price bands as arithmetic with marked hour estimates (and note I'm marking them rather than sourcing to the model, OR actually