              "resultType": "complete",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "1.\ntitle       GitHub - invariantlabs-ai/invariant: Guardrails for secure ...\nhref        https://github.com/invariantlabs-ai/invariant\nbody        Invariant Guardrails is a comprehensive rule-based guardrailing layer for LLM\n            or MCP-powered AI applications. It is deployed between your\n            application and your MCP servers or LLM provider, allowing for\n            continuous steering and monitoring, without invasive code changes.\n2.\ntitle       Guardrails AI vs Invariant Labs — which to pick? · Agentic Stacks\nhref        https://agentic-stacks.com/compare/guardrails-ai-vs-invariant-labs\nbody        Guardrails AI vs Invariant Labs compared: pricing, strengths, tradeoffs, and\n            adoption across real AI agent stacks.\n3.\ntitle       GitHub - macoughl/invariant-guardrails: Guardrails for secure ...\nhref        https://github.com/macoughl/invariant-guardrails\nbody        Invariant Guardrails is a comprehensive rule-based guardrailing layer for LLM\n            or MCP-powered AI applications. It is deployed between your\n            application and your MCP servers or LLM provider, allowing for\n            continuous steering and monitoring, without invasive code changes.\n4.\ntitle       Invariant Labs\nhref        https://invariantlabs.ai/\nbody        We help agent builders create reliable, robust and secure products.\n5.\ntitle       Introducing Guardrails: The contextual security layer for the ...\nhref        https://invariantlabs.ai/blog/guardrails\nbody        Apr 17, 2025 · This is why today, we are releasing Invariant Guardrails, our\n            state-of-the-art guardrailing system for MCP and agentic AI\n            applications. Guardrails is designed to provide a robust and\n            flexible framework for ensuring the safety and reliability of AI\n            agents, with a focus on contextual guardrailing.\n6.\ntitle       Invariant Guardrails - Rule-Based LLM Agent Security | AI/TLDR\nhref        https://ai-tldr.dev/tools/invariant-guardrails/\nbody        To enforce rules on live traffic without code changes, integrate Guardrails\n            through the Invariant Gateway, which evaluates your rules before\n            and after each LLM and MCP request.\n=====Q2=====\n1.\ntitle       Cerbos Hub Pricing | Cerbos\nhref        https://www.cerbos.dev/pricing\nbody        Policy enforcement points via SDK and ORM integrations. ... Support, training,\n            and SLA for production-grade authorization at scale. Custom plans\n            to suit your needs ... \"Principal\" is a term for a user or\n            service. For most applications, the terms \"user\" and \"principal\"\n            are interchangeable because the application is only used by\n            humans. However, Cerbos can also authorize other applications,\n            services, bots or any other non-human identities which access your\n            system.\n2.\ntitle       Authorization for Developers | Policy as Code\nhref        https://www.cerbos.dev/for-developers\nbody        Replace complicated authorization logic with a single API call and allow\n            product owners and security teams to manage access without\n            touching code. ... Replace scattered if/else permission blocks\n            with a single Cerbos SDK call. Define access in policy, enforce it\n            across every app, API, and AI agent - without reimplementing logic\n            per service.\n3.\ntitle       AWS Marketplace: Cerbos Policy Decision Point - Amazon.com\nhref        https://aws.amazon.com/marketplace/pp/prodview-6kkahbtwv3gtq\nbody        Self-hosted policy-as-code authorization layer with REST API and gRPC support\n            on EC2. ... External reviews are from G2 . ... It's very user-\n            friendly, backed by a friendly support team, and a robust\n            playground for testing permission policies. Best of all, Cerbos\n            offers a powerful open-source ...\n4.\ntitle       Cerbos Software Pricing, Alternatives & More 2026 | Capterra\nhref        https://www.capterra.com/p/276390/Cerbos/\nbody        Cerbos offers API-based access controls and permissions, policy management,\n            and role-based permissions for defining authorization rules.\n5.\ntitle       Cerbos Policy Decision Point: Open-Source Authorization Solution | Cerbos\nhref        https://www.cerbos.dev/product-cerbos-pdp\nbody        Decouple data filtering requests from the code via a query plan API, managed\n            by the same policies as application access. ... Cerbos supports\n            integration with any authentication provider.\n6.\ntitle       Cerbos Software Reviews, Demo & Pricing - 2026\nhref        https://www.softwareadvice.com/product/508552-Cerbos/\nbody        Cerbos is an open-source access governance solution that helps businesses\n            manage authorization for applications. It is a policy-based\n            runtime authorization system that allows developers to implement\n            and manage fine-grained access control.\n=====Q3=====\n1.\ntitle       Introducing MCP Tool ACLs in Kong AI Gateway 3.13: Fine-Grained ...\nhref        https://konghq.com/blog/product-releases/mcp-tool-acls-ai-gateway\nbody        Secure your AI agent tools with Kong AI Gateway 3.13's new MCP Tool ACLs.\n            Implement fine-grained, default-deny authorization policies to\n            control access at the tool level.\n2.\ntitle       kong-mcp-gateway-examples/mcp-acls at main · hguerrero/kong ... - GitHub\nhref        https://github.com/hguerrero/kong-mcp-gateway-examples/tree/main/mcp-acls\nbody        MCP Access Control Lists (ACLs) Example This example demonstrates how to use\n            Kong Gateway's ai-mcp-proxy plugin with Access Control Lists\n            (ACLs) to provide fine-grained access control for MCP tools. It\n            shows how to configure different permission levels for various\n            GitHub operations when exposing them to AI agents.\n3.\ntitle       AI MCP Proxy: Configure default and per-tool access control for ...\nhref        https://developer.konghq.com/plugins/ai-mcp-proxy/examples/passthrough-listener-acls/\nbody        Configure default and per-tool access control for passthrough-listener mode\n            MCP servers. For this configuration to work properly, you need to\n            create the following Kong Gateway entities:\n4.\ntitle       MCP Traffic Gateway | Kong Docs\nhref        https://developer.konghq.com/ai-gateway/mcp/\nbody        Bring MCP servers to production securely with AI Gateway AI agents are rapidly\n            becoming core components of modern software, driving the need for\n            structured, reliable interfaces to access tools and data. The\n            Model Context Protocol (MCP) addresses this by enabling agents to\n            reason, plan, and act across services.\n5.\ntitle       developer.konghq.com/app/_how-tos/mcp/use-access-controls-for-mcp-tools ...\nhref        https://github.com/Kong/developer.konghq.com/blob/main/app/_how-tos/mcp/use-access-controls-for-mcp-tools.md\nbody        Configure the AI MCP Proxy plugin Now, let's configure the AI MCP Proxy plugin\n            to apply tool-level access rules. The plugin controls which users\n            or AI agents can see or call each MCP tool. Access is determined\n            by Consumer Groups and individual Consumers using allow and deny\n            lists. A tool ACL replaces the default rule when present.\n6.\ntitle       Model Context Protocol (MCP) Security: How to Restrict Tool Access ...\nhref        https://konghq.com/blog/engineering/mcp-tool-governance-security-meets-context-efficiency\nbody        Secure your AI agents with advanced MCP Tool Governance. Learn how gateway-\n            level ACLs (Access Control Lists) solve over-permissioning,\n            eliminate Context Rot, and boost LLM performance in production.",
                  "annotations": {
                    "priority": 0.0
                  }
                }
              ],
              "structuredContent": {
                "stdout": "1.\ntitle       GitHub - invariantlabs-ai/invariant: Guardrails for secure ...\nhref        https://github.com/invariantlabs-ai/invariant\nbody        Invariant Guardrails is a comprehensive rule-based guardrailing layer for LLM\n            or MCP-powered AI applications. It is deployed between your\n            application and your MCP servers or LLM provider, allowing for\n            continuous steering and monitoring, without invasive code changes.\n2.\ntitle       Guardrails AI vs Invariant Labs — which to pick? · Agentic Stacks\nhref        https://agentic-stacks.com/compare/guardrails-ai-vs-invariant-labs\nbody        Guardrails AI vs Invariant Labs compared: pricing, strengths, tradeoffs, and\n            adoption across real AI agent stacks.\n3.\ntitle       GitHub - macoughl/invariant-guardrails: Guardrails for secure ...\nhref        https://github.com/macoughl/invariant-guardrails\nbody        Invariant Guardrails is a comprehensive rule-based guardrailing layer for LLM\n            or MCP-powered AI applications. It is deployed between your\n            application and your MCP servers or LLM provider, allowing for\n            continuous steering and monitoring, without invasive code changes.\n4.\ntitle       Invariant Labs\nhref        https://invariantlabs.ai/\nbody        We help agent builders create reliable, robust and secure products.\n5.\ntitle       Introducing Guardrails: The contextual security layer for the ...\nhref        https://invariantlabs.ai/blog/guardrails\nbody        Apr 17, 2025 · This is why today, we are releasing Invariant Guardrails, our\n            state-of-the-art guardrailing system for MCP and agentic AI\n            applications. Guardrails is designed to provide a robust and\n            flexible framework for ensuring the safety and reliability of AI\n            agents, with a focus on contextual guardrailing.\n6.\ntitle       Invariant Guardrails - Rule-Based LLM Agent Security | AI/TLDR\nhref        https://ai-tldr.dev/tools/invariant-guardrails/\nbody        To enforce rules on live traffic without code changes, integrate Guardrails\n            through the Invariant Gateway, which evaluates your rules before\n            and after each LLM and MCP request.\n=====Q2=====\n1.\ntitle       Cerbos Hub Pricing | Cerbos\nhref        https://www.cerbos.dev/pricing\nbody        Policy enforcement points via SDK and ORM integrations. ... Support, training,\n            and SLA for production-grade authorization at scale. Custom plans\n            to suit your needs ... \"Principal\" is a term for a user or\n            service. For most applications, the terms \"user\" and \"principal\"\n            are interchangeable because the application is only used by\n            humans. However, Cerbos can also authorize other applications,\n            services, bots or any other non-human identities which access your\n            system.\n2.\ntitle       Authorization for Developers | Policy as Code\nhref        https://www.cerbos.dev/for-developers\nbody        Replace complicated authorization logic with a single API call and allow\n            product owners and security teams to manage access without\n            touching code. ... Replace scattered if/else permission blocks\n            with a single Cerbos SDK call. Define access in policy, enforce it\n            across every app, API, and AI agent - without reimplementing logic\n            per service.\n3.\ntitle       AWS Marketplace: Cerbos Policy Decision Point - Amazon.com\nhref        https://aws.amazon.com/marketplace/pp/prodview-6kkahbtwv3gtq\nbody        Self-hosted policy-as-code authorization layer with REST API and gRPC support\n            on EC2. ... External reviews are from G2 . ... It's very user-\n            friendly, backed by a friendly support team, and a robust\n            playground for testing permission policies. Best of all, Cerbos\n            offers a powerful open-source ...\n4.\ntitle       Cerbos Software Pricing, Alternatives & More 2026 | Capterra\nhref        https://www.capterra.com/p/276390/Cerbos/\nbody        Cerbos offers API-based access controls and permissions, policy management,\n            and role-based permissions for defining authorization rules.\n5.\ntitle       Cerbos Policy Decision Point: Open-Source Authorization Solution | Cerbos\nhref        https://www.cerbos.dev/product-cerbos-pdp\nbody        Decouple data filtering requests from the code via a query plan API, managed\n            by the same policies as application access. ... Cerbos supports\n            integration with any authentication provider.\n6.\ntitle       Cerbos Software Reviews, Demo & Pricing - 2026\nhref        https://www.softwareadvice.com/product/508552-Cerbos/\nbody        Cerbos is an open-source access governance solution that helps businesses\n            manage authorization for applications. It is a policy-based\n            runtime authorization system that allows developers to implement\n            and manage fine-grained access control.\n=====Q3=====\n1.\ntitle       Introducing MCP Tool ACLs in Kong AI Gateway 3.13: Fine-Grained ...\nhref        https://konghq.com/blog/product-releases/mcp-tool-acls-ai-gateway\nbody        Secure your AI agent tools with Kong AI Gateway 3.13's new MCP Tool ACLs.\n            Implement fine-grained, default-deny authorization policies to\n            control access at the tool level.\n2.\ntitle       kong-mcp-gateway-examples/mcp-acls at main · hguerrero/kong ... - GitHub\nhref        https://github.com/hguerrero/kong-mcp-gateway-examples/tree/main/mcp-acls\nbody        MCP Access Control Lists (ACLs) Example This example demonstrates how to use\n            Kong Gateway's ai-mcp-proxy plugin with Access Control Lists\n            (ACLs) to provide fine-grained access control for MCP tools. It\n            shows how to configure different permission levels for various\n            GitHub operations when exposing them to AI agents.\n3.\ntitle       AI MCP Proxy: Configure default and per-tool access control for ...\nhref        https://developer.konghq.com/plugins/ai-mcp-proxy/examples/passthrough-listener-acls/\nbody        Configure default and per-tool access control for passthrough-listener mode\n            MCP servers. For this configuration to work properly, you need to\n            create the following Kong Gateway entities:\n4.\ntitle       MCP Traffic Gateway | Kong Docs\nhref        https://developer.konghq.com/ai-gateway/mcp/\nbody        Bring MCP servers to production securely with AI Gateway AI agents are rapidly\n            becoming core components of modern software, driving the need for\n            structured, reliable interfaces to access tools and data. The\n            Model Context Protocol (MCP) addresses this by enabling agents to\n            reason, plan, and act across services.\n5.\ntitle       developer.konghq.com/app/_how-tos/mcp/use-access-controls-for-mcp-tools ...\nhref        https://github.com/Kong/developer.konghq.com/blob/main/app/_how-tos/mcp/use-access-controls-for-mcp-tools.md\nbody        Configure the AI MCP Proxy plugin Now, let's configure the AI MCP Proxy plugin\n            to apply tool-level access rules. The plugin controls which users\n            or AI agents can see or call each MCP tool. Access is determined\n            by Consumer Groups and individual Consumers using allow and deny\n            lists. A tool ACL replaces the default rule when present.\n6.\ntitle       Model Context Protocol (MCP) Security: How to Restrict Tool Access ...\nhref        https://konghq.com/blog/engineering/mcp-tool-governance-security-meets-context-efficiency\nbody        Secure your AI agents with advanced MCP Tool Governance. Learn how gateway-\n            level ACLs (Access Control Lists) solve over-permissioning,\n            eliminate Context Rot, and boost LLM performance in production.",
