          "thinking": "The outward search confirms the market context:\n- Invariant Labs (invariantlabs.ai) sells agent guardrails/security — a funded startup, free OSS + gateway.\n- Cerbos sells policy-as-code, open-source + Cerbos Hub (paid).\n- Kong AI Gateway 3.13 ships MCP Tool ACLs (default-deny) — free/OSS, commoditizing the guarded-MCP layer.\n\nSo the market for \"constrain what an agent can do / prove what it did\" is real and being serviced by funded/OSS vendors. That's the strongest case against: umbra and acompose are competing against funded startups and free OSS gateway features, from a solo operator with no customer base.\n\nLet me do one more search on the acompose side — agent personas/specialists — to check whether harness vendors ship this natively (commoditizing acompose). And maybe a search on \"agent consulting retainer pricing\" or similar to ground the support retainer. Actually, let me also check Invariant Labs pricing to get a comparable number for the \"would anyone pay\" question.\n\nActually, I have enough for the market claim. Let me get one more data point: acompose comparables (agent subagents/personas native in harnesses) and maybe Invariant pricing.\n\nLet me do a focused second batch:\n1. \"Claude Code subagents personalities agent compose\" or \"OpenCode Codex custom agent specialists\"\n2. \"Invariant Labs pricing\" to get a comparable price signal.\n\nActually, I think I have enough to make the case. The buy/no-buy verdict should go to Jev anyway. Let me not over-search. One more quick search for acompose native-in-harness to nail the \"commoditized\" claim, and Invariant pricing for a comparable number.\n\nLet me do that quickly.",
