      "content": [
        {
          "type": "thinking",
          "thinking": "Now I have strong market context:\n\n**Comparables for the buy/no-buy:**\n- **umbra's value** (\"constrain what an agent can do, prove what it did\") is directly serviced by:\n  - Invariant Labs (invariantlabs.ai) — funded startup, \"state-of-the-art guardrailing system for MCP and agentic AI\", free OSS + gateway.\n  - Cerbos — open-source policy-as-code + Cerbos Hub (paid).\n  - Kong AI Gateway 3.13 — MCP Tool ACLs, default-deny, free/OSS.\n- **acompose's value** (\"materialize a specialist on any harness\") is serviced natively by:\n  - Claude Code subagents (YAML frontmatter, tool restrictions, model selection), and 75-subagent public repos. Codex/Goose/OpenCode all have native equivalent.\n  - This is commoditized: harness vendors ship it for free.\n\n**Market pricing for the support/retainer:**\n- AI agent retainer: $1,000–$5,000/mo for solo consultants/small shops (Duet).\n- AI agent maintenance: monitoring from ~$500/mo, maintenance+engineering up to several thousand/mo.\n- Consultant/agency deployed agent: $300–$1,500/mo per client (Pickaxe).\n- Project engagement for building workflows: $3,000–$8,000 (theaiconsultingnetwork).\n\nSo the market has real, published price points. And the rule rate ($150/hr) is confirmed.\n\nNow, the strongest case against anyone buying:\n1. umbra competes head-on with funded Invariant Labs and free Kong MCP ACLs + Cerbos — the \"constrain/prove\" category is already served, much of it free/OSS. The differentiated piece (execve/argv boundary, one-file review, per-verb scope tokens) is real but a solo operator can't out-market a funded startup, and the buyer who needs execve-level (not MCP-tool-level) policy is rare and sophisticated.\n2. acompose competes with free, native harness subagents (Claude Code YAML subagents, 75-subagent public repos). The differentiated piece (measured roster, voice changes behavior, cross-harness portability) is a nice-to-have, not a must-pay.\n3. No customer base, no flagship, single maintainer — support contract from a solo operator is supply risk. Portfolio doc explicitly forbids claiming customers.\n4. The buyer for \"custom one-time cut + support\" of OSS tooling is a niche: a team already running agents against their own infra who wants a bespoke policy integration. That's a small, technical slice, and they can often do it in-house.\n\nThe reversal condition: a warm introduction to a specific buyer with a compliance/audit need (defense/IC chain where umbra \"ships no model\" is sayable, or a security team needing execve-level proof), who lacks in-house bandwidth and can't use off-the-shelf ACLs because they need policy at the syscall boundary. One converted paid pilot flips \"no one buys.\"\n\nNow, per the guardrail, I must source the verdict (go/no-go, and the numeric price/duration) to the decision model (Jev), not generate it myself.\n\nLet me formulate the Jev questions. I'll do:\n\n1. **noul** for umbra: \"Is there at least one realistic paying buyer for a custom one-time cut + long-term support of umbra?\"\n2. **noul** for acompose: same.\n\nActually, \"would anyone actually buy one\" is the go/no-go. Two noul questions, or one. Let me do two noul (one per product) since the answer could differ.\n\nAlso, per the guardrail, the price numbers should also go to the model. But price bands are ranges I derive from the confirmed rate + hour scope. Let me think: should I send the price-band magnitude to Jev as a choice/score?\n\nI think the cleanest compliant approach:\n- The buy/no-buy (go/no-go): send to Jev (noul ×2).\n- The price bands: these are arithmetic from the CONFIRMED $150/hr rate. The only estimate is hours-per-scope and retainer hours/mo. I'll present the price bands as arithmetic, clearly labeling the hour assumptions as my own estimate (the guardrail allows marking \"this seat's own unchecked estimate\" — but wait, it says where the model is available I should use it).\n\nLet me reconsider. The guardrail says every price/duration goes to the model. I have the model. But Jev answers \"only the typed shape it was asked\" — it can't freeform-generate a dollar band. It can do a \"choice\" (pick one option) or \"score\" (ordered level). So I can frame the price band as a choice question with symmetric options, and Jev picks.\n\nBut honestly, the price band is better served by arithmetic: rate ($150, confirmed) × hours. The hours depend on scope which is a product-engineering fact, not really a \"decision model\" question. I think the more defensible move is:\n\n- Send the go/no-go (buy demand) to Jev — this is the load-bearing verdict the user asked for (\"would anyone actually buy\").\n- Compute price bands as arithmetic from the confirmed rate, with hour ranges as my clearly-marked scope estimate, and cross-check the resulting monthly retainer against the market numbers I just found ($500–$5,000/mo retainer range, which my $150/hr-derived number should land inside).\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether to also send a price-band question to Jev. The user explicitly asked \"give me price bands.\" If I send price to the model as a choice with options, I'm outsourcing my scope analysis. That seems wrong — the scope/hours is my analysis work. The go/no-go is the market verdict.\n\nI'll compromise cleanly:\n- Price bands: I compute from the confirmed $150/hr rate, present as low/mid/high scope bands with explicit hour assumptions. These are deterministic arithmetic once hours are set; I mark the hour assumptions as my estimate (not a model verdict). This is transparent and compliant (the guardrail's own escape hatch: mark as own estimate; and \"arithmetic that is deterministic stays local\").\n- Buy/no-buy: send to Jev as noul, because it's a genuine go/no-go probability call, and report the model's answer + confidence.\n\nActually, let me re-read the guardrail one more time on this: \"Source the verdict outside this seat. Every probability, likelihood, numeric estimate (a price, a count, a duration), ranking, and go or no-go goes to the deployment's decision model as a typed question.\"\n\nHmm, \"a price\" is explicitly listed. But then \"arithmetic that is deterministic stays local.\" The price = rate × hours. The rate is confirmed. The hours is the only free variable. So the price is NOT deterministic until I pick hours. \n\nI think the honest, useful answer: I'll send BOTH the go/no-go AND a price-band signal to Jev, because the user asked for both and the guardrail wants both sourced. For price, I'll frame it as a \"choice\" question with symmetric options (which price band is realistic for a first-year one-time-cut+support deal). Then report Jev's pick, and ALSO show the arithmetic bands so the user has the number.\n\nActually, this risks over-engineering. Let me be decisive and do what the guardrail minimally requires and what's most useful:\n\n1. Jev noul ×2 (buy demand for umbra, acompose) — the go/no-go.\n2. Present price bands as arithmetic from confirmed $150/hr, with hour-scope assumptions clearly marked, cross-checked against retrieved market retainer ranges.\n\nFor the price bands, I'll ALSO note that these are my own scope estimates (marked as such), not sourced to the model, and say so plainly (per the guardrail's \"say so\" clause).\n\nLet me now design the Jev questions carefully per the guardrail's neutrality requirement:\n\n**State** (shared context, carries primary sources + evidence both sides, no verdict):\n\nFor the noul question, I need `instructions` and optionally `criteria` {true, false}. The state is an object.\n\nLet me write the state with evidence on both sides:\n- Product: umbra (occlusion engine, execve/argv policy, per-verb scope tokens, egress proxy, audit log, exit-code taxonomy, \"one file a reviewer reads in a sitting\").\n- Operator: solo, $150/hr rule rate, no confirmed customer base or flagship (portfolio doc).\n- Market: Invariant Labs (funded startup) sells MCP/agentic guardrails; Kong AI Gateway 3.13 ships MCP Tool ACLs default-deny (free/OSS); Cerbos sells policy-as-code (OSS + paid hub).\n- umbra's differentiation: policy at the execve/argv boundary (deeper than MCP tool ACLs), no denylist, audit log + exit-code taxonomy, no model shipped (sayable in defense/IC procurement under NDAA §1532 covered-AI).\n- Buyer profile: a team running agents against its own systems with a compliance/audit need, or a defense/IC-chain contractor.\n\nFor acompose:\n- Product: agent-compose renders housecast bundles into harness surfaces (Claude Code, Codex, Goose, OpenCode); one command materializes a named specialist; measured roster; voice changes behavior.\n- Market: Claude Code ships native subagents (YAML frontmatter, tool restrictions, model selection); public repos with 75+ subagents; Codex/Goose/OpenCode have native equivalents.\n- Differentiation: cross-harness portability, roster measured/validated via housecast evals, personality changes what the agent notices/refuses.\n\nNow, the noul question format from the schema:\n```\nquestions: {\n  \"umbra_buy\": {\n    \"type\": \"noul\",\n    \"instructions\": \"...\",\n    \"criteria\": { \"true\": \"...\", \"false\": \"...\" }  // optional\n  }\n}\n```\n\nLet me look at the schema again:\n\ncreate_noul_decision:\n- model (string)\n- questions: object, keyed, each anyOf: {type \"noul\", instructions (string), criteria {true, false} optional}\n- state: object (raw)\n\nThe criteria is optional. Let me include instructions and criteria for clarity.\n\nActually, the criteria {true, false} — what does that mean? Probably labels for what true/false would mean in this context, i.e., \"true\" = \"yes there is a buyer\", \"false\" = \"no there isn't\". Let me set those.\n\nLet me write the instructions neutrally:\n\numbra noul instructions: \"Given the product, operator, and market context in state, is there at least one realistic paying buyer for a custom one-time cut plus long-term support of umbra within the next 12 months?\"\n\ncriteria: true = \"Yes, at least one realistic paying buyer\", false = \"No, no realistic paying buyer\".\n\nacompose noul instructions: same for acompose.\n\nLet me also decide the model: \"jev-latest\".\n\nNow let me write the state object. It should carry the evidence, not my verdict. It should be neutral — someone reading state alone can't tell which answer I expect.\n\nLet me construct the state carefully and neutrally.\n\nState:\n```json\n{\n  \"operator\": {\n    \"size\": \"solo maintainer\",\n    \"rule_rate_usd_per_hour\": 150,\n    \"confirmed_customer_base\": \"none on record\",\n    \"flagship\": \"none established\"\n  },\n  \"umbra\": {\n    \"what\": \"occlusion engine; CLI enforcing agent tool policy at argv/execve boundary\",\n    \"components\": \"argv validated before execve; per-verb scope token; egress via per-invocation proxy; appended audit log; published exit-code taxonomy separating policy refusal from tool failure\",\n    \"differentiation\": \"policy at syscall/execve boundary rather than MCP tool level; ships no model\",\n    \"procurement_note\": \"ships no model, so unaffected by NDAA Section 1532 covered-AI restriction\"\n  },\n  \"acompose\": {\n    \"what\": \"renders a validated roster bundle into harness surfaces without executable authority\",\n    \"components\": \"one command materializes a named specialist with voice, handoff set, tool inventory; runs unchanged on Claude Code, Codex, Goose, OpenCode\",\n    \"differentiation\": \"cross-harness portability; roster measured by eval board; personality changes what agent notices and refuses\"\n  },\n  \"market_comparables\": {\n    \"umbra_space\": [\n      \"Invariant Labs: funded startup selling rule-based guardrails for MCP/agentic apps\",\n      \"Kong AI Gateway 3.13: ships MCP Tool ACLs, default-deny, free/OSS\",\n      \"Cerbos: open-source policy-as-code plus paid Cerbos Hub\"\n    ],\n    \"acompose_space\": [\n      \"Claude Code ships native subagents via YAML frontmatter with tool restrictions and model selection\",\n      \"public repos distribute 75+ specialized subagents free\",\n      \"Codex, Goose, OpenCode each have native specialist/agent equivalents\"\n    ],\n    \"retainer_market_usd_per_month\": \"solo-consultant AI-agent retainers commonly $1,000-$5,000/mo; basic monitoring from ~$500/mo\"\n  },\n  \"offer_shape\": \"one-time custom cut (setup/integration/config) plus recurring long-term support retainer\"\n}\n```\n\nThat's neutral and evidence-carrying.\n\nNow let me make the two noul calls. I can do them in one create_noul_decision call with two questions, or two separate calls. The schema's `questions` is an object keyed by name, so I can put both questions in one call. Let me do one call with both questions.\n\nActually, looking more carefully at the schema for questions — it's `\"additionalProperties\": {\"anyOf\": [...]}` meaning each key maps to one of the question shapes. So I can do:\n```json\n\"questions\": {\n  \"umbra_buy_demand\": { \"type\": \"noul\", \"instructions\": \"...\", \"criteria\": {...} },\n  \"acompose_buy_demand\": { \"type\": \"noul\", \"instructions\": \"...\", \"criteria\": {...} }\n}\n```\n\nLet me do it. Also there's a `state` object.\n\nLet me write the tool call.\n\nActually, wait — let me reconsider whether to also ask a score/choice for the price band. Given the guardrail lists \"a price\" as needing the model, and I want to be compliant, let me ALSO add a score or choice question for the realistic price band.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: the user asked for \"price bands\" (plural, a range), for each product. The most natural model call for \"which price band is realistic\" is a `choice` with symmetric options. But the options I'd give would encode bands, and choosing symmetric neutral options is tricky.\n\nAlternatively, I can treat the price band as deterministic arithmetic (rate × hours) where hours is my scope estimate, and NOT send to the model, instead clearly marking it. The guardrail's escape: \"Where the deployment names no decision model, or the call fails, say so, and mark the number as this seat's own unchecked estimate.\" But I DO have a model. However, the spirit is: numeric estimates that are genuinely uncertain go to the model. A price band built from a confirmed rate + my scope estimate is a mix. The genuinely uncertain part (hours) is my scope analysis, which is exactly what I'm paid to do as the director.\n\nI'll make a judgment call: send the go/no-go to Jev (clearly required), and present price bands as arithmetic with clearly-marked hour assumptions. I'll note plainly that the rate is confirmed ($150/hr) and the hour-scope and retainer assumptions are my estimate, not a model verdict. This is honest and useful.\n\nActually, let me reconsider once more. I could add a single score question to Jev asking for the \"realistic first-year total price magnitude\" on an ordered scale, to have the model weigh in on whether my arithmetic lands in a reasonable zone. But score is \"ordered level\" — I'd define criteria like [\"<$5k\", \"$5k-$15k\", \"$15k-$40k\", \"$40k-$100k\", \">$100k\"] as ordered levels. Actually the score tool takes `criteria` as a list of 2-10 items (ordered levels). So a score question with those 5 ordered levels, asking \"where does a realistic first-year custom-cut+support price for umbra land?\" That would give me a model-sourced price magnitude.\n\nHmm, but that's one score question, and it produces an ordered level pick. That's actually a clean way to satisfy \"price goes to the model.\" Let me do it.\n\nLet me design:\n\nscore question \"umbra_first_year_price_band\":\n- criteria: [\"under $5k\", \"$5k-$15k\", \"$15k-$40k\", \"$40k-$100k\", \"over $100k\"]\n- instructions: \"For a custom one-time cut plus long-term support of umbra, sold by the solo operator described in state at a $150/hr rule rate, which first-year total price band is most realistic for a converting buyer?\"\n\nAnd acompose equivalent.\n\nActually, the score tool criteria is \"an ordered level\" — the criteria items are the ordered levels. Good.\n\nBut wait — do I want to muddy this? The user asked for concrete price bands, and I have the arithmetic. Sending the magnitude to Jev gives me a model-sourced anchor to compare against my arithmetic. That's actually the outward/decisive thing to do.\n\nLet me do both: noul (buy/no-buy) + score (price magnitude). Both per product. That's 2 noul + 2 score, or I can do it in 2 calls (one per product) or combined.\n\nActually, let me combine all four questions. But noul and score are different tool schemas (create_noul_decision vs create_score_decision). So I need two tool calls: one create_noul_decision with 2 noul questions, one create_score_decision with 2 score questions.\n\nLet me do both calls in parallel (they're independent).\n\nLet me write them.\n\nFor create_noul_decision:\n- model: \"jev-latest\"\n- questions: umbra_buy_demand, acompose_buy_demand (noul each)\n- state: the neutral state\n\nFor create_score_decision:\n- model: \"jev-latest\"\n- questions: umbra_first_year_price, acompose_first_year_price (score each with criteria list)\n- state: the neutral state (same)\n\nLet me write the criteria for score. The score criteria must be min 2 max 10 items. I'll use 5 ordered levels.\n\nLet me write the full state as a single object reused in both calls.\n\nLet me now make the calls.\n\nActually, one concern: the `state` field has `\"x-opcore-raw\": true` and `\"type\": \"object\"` with no required properties, and it's a raw object. I'll pass a JSON object.\n\nLet me write the state compactly but completely.\n\nLet me now produce the tool calls. I'll write the state inline for both.\n\nLet me draft the state JSON:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"operator\": \"solo maintainer, $150/hr rule rate, no customer base or flagship on record\",\n  \"offer\": \"one-time custom cut (setup/integration/config) plus recurring long-term support retainer\",\n  \"umbra\": {\n    \"description\": \"occlusion engine CLI; agent tool policy enforced at argv/execve boundary\",\n    \"capabilities\": \"argv validated before execve; per-verb scope token; egress through per-invocation proxy; appended audit log; exit-code taxonomy separating policy refusal from tool failure; one-file review\",\n    \"differentiation\": \"policy at syscall/execve boundary rather than MCP tool level; ships no model; unaffected by NDAA Section 1532 covered-AI restriction\"\n  },\n  \"acompose\": {\n    \"description\": \"renders a validated roster bundle into harness surfaces without executable authority\",\n    \"capabilities\": \"one command materializes a named specialist with voice, handoff set, tool inventory; runs unchanged on Claude Code, Codex, Goose, OpenCode\",\n    \"differentiation\": \"cross-harness portability; roster measured by eval board; personality changes what the agent notices and refuses\"\n  },\n  \"umbra_market\": [\"Invariant Labs sells rule-based guardrails for MCP/agentic apps (funded startup)\", \"Kong AI Gateway 3.13 ships MCP Tool ACLs default-deny, free/OSS\", \"Cerbos open-source policy-as-code plus paid Cerbos Hub\"],\n  \"acompose_market\": [\"Claude Code ships native subagents via YAML frontmatter with tool restrictions and model selection\", \"public repos distribute 75+ specialized subagents free\", \"Codex, Goose, OpenCode each have native specialist/agent equivalents\"],\n  \"retainer_market\": \"solo-consultant AI-agent retainers commonly $1,000-$5,000/mo; basic monitoring from about $500/mo\"\n}\n```\n\nGood, neutral, evidence on both sides.\n\nNow instructions for noul:\n- umbra: \"Weigh the umbra description, its market comparables, and the operator/offer facts in state. Is there at least one realistic paying buyer for this one-time cut plus long-term support within the next 12 months?\"\n- acompose: \"Weigh the acompose description, its market comparables, and the operator/offer facts in state. Is there at least one realistic paying buyer for this one-time cut plus long-term support within the next 12 months?\"\n\ncriteria:\n- true: \"At least one realistic paying buyer\"\n- false: \"No realistic paying buyer\"\n\nFor score:\n- criteria: [\"under $5,000\", \"$5,000 to $15,000\", \"$15,000 to $40,000\", \"$40,000 to $100,000\", \"over $100,000\"]\n- instructions: \"For a converting buyer of this product under the offer in state, which first-year total (one-time cut plus support) price band is most realistic at the operator's $150/hr rate?\"\n\nLet me fire both tool calls in parallel.",
