          "text": "## The call\n\nDemand is a coin flip, and only umbra clears it. Someone *might* buy a custom cut of umbra from a compliance or defense-chain buyer, in the $15k–$40k first-year band. Acompose has no standalone buyer at a supportable price; treat it as a bolt-on to a housecast sale, not its own offer.\n\n## The strongest case against, first\n\nBoth of these are sold by a solo operator with no customer base and no flagship on record, and each one's core value is already free or funded outside this repo. I checked rather than assumed:\n\n- **umbra's category is occupied.** Invariant Labs sells rule-based guardrails for MCP/agentic apps as a funded startup. Kong AI Gateway 3.13 ships MCP Tool ACLs with default-deny, free/OSS. Cerbos sells open-source policy-as-code plus a paid hub. The differentiating piece, policy at the argv/execve boundary rather than the MCP-tool boundary, is real but it is a *rarer, harder* sale, not an easier one. And the natural product in this line, mcp-beaver, was already closed as a product (2026-09-02) because Kong commoditized exactly the layer it guarded.\n- **acompose is commoditized by the harnesses it renders into.** Claude Code ships native subagents via YAML frontmatter with tool restrictions and model selection; public repos distribute 75+ specialized subagents free; Codex, Goose, and OpenCode each have a native equivalent. \"Materialize a specialist\" is now table stakes, not a line item.\n- **A support contract from a solo maintainer is supply risk a buyer prices in**, and the buyer for a bespoke cut of OSS tooling is a narrow technical slice who can usually self-serve.\n\n## What would reverse it\n\nOne warm introduction that converts: a named buyer with a compliance/audit need or a defense/IC-chain position (umbra ships no model, so it stays sayable where sirens-echo's Deep is covered-AI), who needs policy at the syscall boundary, not the MCP-tool boundary, and lacks in-house capacity. A single paid pilot flips \"nobody buys\" to \"someone buys.\" For acompose, the reversal is a buyer already committed to housecast who wants the rendered specialist on a harness they cannot configure themselves.\n\n## The model's verdict\n\nSourced to Jev (jev-1.13.0), not generated here:\n\n| Question | Verdict | Read |\n|---|---|---|\n| umbra buy demand | 0.60 (0–1, above 0.5 = yes) | weak yes, coin flip |\n| acompose buy demand | 0.58 | weak yes, coin flip |\n| umbra first-year price | score 1.68 | $15k–$40k (55%), $5k–$15k (33%) |\n| acompose first-year price | score 1.44 | $5k–$15k (53%), $15k–$40k (39%) |\n\nConfidence on every call was 0.56–0.60, which is the model saying \"marginal, do not treat as conviction.\"\n\n## Price bands\n\nThe rate is confirmed at $150/hr. The hour assumptions below are my scope estimate, not a model verdict; multiply them by the rate to move the band.\n\n| | One-time cut | Monthly support | First-year total |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **umbra** | $3,000–$12,000 (20–80 hrs) | $900–$2,250 (6–15 hrs/mo) | **$15,000–$40,000** |\n| **acompose** | $1,500–$6,000 (10–40 hrs) | $450–$1,200 (3–8 hrs/mo) | **$5,000–$15,000** |\n\nThe market I retrieved backs these: solo-consultant AI-agent retainers run $1,000–$5,000/mo, basic monitoring from about $500/mo. My $150/hr-derived retainers land at the low end of that, which is honest for an unproven solo operator.\n\n## Would anyone actually buy one\n\n**Umbra: maybe, one buyer, at the bottom of its band.** The defense/compliance slice is real and the no-model fact is a genuine wedge, but it is a single narrow buyer reached only through a warm introduction, and the model's 0.60 with 0.60 confidence is not a business, it is a pilot.\n\n**Acompose: not as a standalone.** Fold it into a housecast sale as the rendered output; do not price or pitch it alone. There is no buyer who pays to be told to use the harness's own subagent feature.\n\nThe gate is this: umbra gets one shot at a warm intro to a compliance/defense buyer before